{"text": " Being smart can make you stupid. Consider two men with the same ambition. The first spends months studying every possible outcome, calculating risks and preparing for problems he has never encountered. Convinced that the more he understands, the more likely he is to succeed. The second begins with an imperfect plan. He makes mistakes and counters obstacles he never anticipated and fails repeatedly. But every failure reveals something the first man has only imagined. And after a year, the difference becomes impossible to ignore. The first man understands 100 reasons why his idea might fail. The second has discovered which of those reasons actually mattered. And this is what we might call the intelligence trap. The belief that understanding every possible consequence is more valuable than confronting reality itself. Because intelligence can help you anticipate what might happen, but experience reveals what actually happens. And sometimes, the man who knows less achieves more. Not because ignorance makes him capable, but because he was willing to begin before he knew everything.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.48, "text": " Being smart can make you stupid.", "tokens": [50364, 8891, 4069, 393, 652, 291, 6631, 13, 50488], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10457448126042931, "compression_ratio": 1.6725978647686832, "no_speech_prob": 0.11241201311349869}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 2.48, "end": 4.88, "text": " Consider two men with the same ambition.", "tokens": [50488, 17416, 732, 1706, 365, 264, 912, 22814, 13, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10457448126042931, "compression_ratio": 1.6725978647686832, "no_speech_prob": 0.11241201311349869}, {"id": 2, "seek": 0, "start": 4.88, "end": 8.64, "text": " The first spends months studying every possible outcome,", "tokens": [50608, 440, 700, 25620, 2493, 7601, 633, 1944, 9700, 11, 50796], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10457448126042931, "compression_ratio": 1.672